575: relative to \(V\) is
576: 
577: \[
578:  H_a=(W\cup_FD^3)\times S^1
579:       =D^2_a\times S^1_b\times S^1_\gamma.
580: \]
581: 
582: The subscripts retain the marked boundary cycles.  This cap kills
583: \(a\).  The usual elliptic cap kills \(\gamma\); their boundary
584: markings are not silently equated.  Both have signature zero.
585: 
586: Delete the normal disc bundle of \(X\) from \(Z\), and glue
587: \((C\times S^2)\times I\) along the resulting boundary collar.
588: The resulting two closed boundary components are the physical
589: positive return family \(P_f\) and the normal sphere bundle
590: \(B=S(\bar\nu)\to\bar X\).  On the outer side this is precisely
591: the replacement of the fixed \(F\times D^3\) by \(D^3\times S^2\),
592: performed in every parameter fibre.  On the inner side it completes
593: the normal sphere bundle over the cap.  We now specify its two
594: boundary orientations and the return convention explicitly.
595: 
596: Let the circle-first quotient convention be
597: \(\Omega_{X_6}=d\delta\wedge\Omega_Y\).  In the fixed normal
598: complex two-plane the weights are \((-1,+1)\).  Conjugating the
599: negative-weight complex coordinate reverses its real orientation;
600: contracting the positive Hopf volume by the circle vector reverses
601: the outward cone convention once more.  The two signs give
602: \(\Omega_Y=\Omega_F\wedge\Omega_{\nu,\mathrm{Hopf}}\), where
603: the meridian sphere has the positive Hopf orientation.  The graph
604: part of \(X\) has the complex page orientation, and the oriented
605: compression has \(\partial X=F\times S^1_\theta\), in that order.
606: Give \(Z=Y\times D^2\) its product orientation.  Since \(\dim Y=5\),
607: the outward boundary orientation is
608: 
609: \[
610:  \Omega_{\partial Z}=-\Omega_Y\wedge d\theta.
611: \]
612: 
613: The inner boundary of the deleted normal disc bundle is oriented
614: \(-\Omega_{\bar X}\wedge\Omega_{S^2}\).  The product cap preserves
615: these signs.  Thus the actual oriented bordism has boundary
616: \(-P_f\sqcup(-B)\), with \(P_f\) oriented by the fibre followed
617: by positive parameter, and \(B\) by the complex page followed
618: by the positive meridian sphere.
619: 
620: Jin's mapping torus \(M_f\) identifies \((z,0)\sim(f(z),1)\).
621: The physical positive family of the return \(f\), which identifies
622: \((z,1)\sim(f(z),0)\), is therefore \(P_f=M_{f^{-1}}\).
623: Characteristic-number additivity under inversion, together with
624: the two boundary signs just computed, gives
625: 
626: \[
627:  (k,\ell,A)(P_f)=-(k,\ell,A)(B),\qquad
628:  (k,\ell,A)_{\mathrm{Jin}}(f)=(k,\ell,A)(B).
629: \]
630: 
631: This records both reversals.  The following integrations on \(B\)
632: therefore compute Jin's invariants of the specified physical
633: positive return \(f\), with the stated positive meridian class.
634: 
635: The Euler line extends over the bordism.  On
636: \(F\times S^1\times S^2\) its Chern class is the meridian generator,
637: by the positive-weight SU(2) trivialization of P3F.7.  It therefore
638: extends over \(D^3\times S^1\times S^2\).  An ambiguity in the
639: boundary bundle isomorphism is a map to \(S^1\); its homotopy class
640: lies in \(H^1(S^2\times S^1\times S^2)=\mathbf Z\), and the
641: restriction from \(H^1(D^3\times S^1\times S^2)\) is an isomorphism.
642: It extends as well.  The bordism is spin: \(Y\times D^2\) is spin,
643: and the two possible spin structures on the gluing boundary differ
644: only on the same \(S^1\) factor, where either extends over the cap.
645: This identifies the actual spin return symbol and Euler line with
